  "howToTitle": "A small exercise for a busy mind",
  "howToIntro": "Cognitive shuffling gives your mind unrelated, easy-to-picture words. You are not trying to force sleep. You are giving racing thoughts nothing to hold on to.",
  "howToStep1": "Lie down and dim the room. Put the phone down; the words keep going.",
  "howToStep2": "Let one concrete word arrive. Picture it as a simple scene, without judging the scene.",
  "howToStep3": "Do not connect the words into a story. Let each scene appear, then fade.",
  "howToStep4": "When a thought pulls you away, notice it and return to the next word.",
  "howToStep5": "Keep the voice low. The sleep timer fades the words out so you can stop paying attention.",
  "howToWhyTitle": "Why it may help",
  "howToWhy": "The method is also called serial diverse imagining. It fills working memory with random, emotionally neutral images, making it harder for the mind to keep rehearsing worried thoughts, one of the main drivers of pre-sleep arousal. The evidence is early and limited; this is not a treatment for chronic insomnia.",
  "howToReferenceTitle": "Study reference",
  "howToReference": "Digdon, N., & Beaudoin, L. P. (2015). A test of the somnolent mentation theory and the cognitive shuffle insomnia treatment. Poster presented at the 37th Annual Conference of the Cognitive Science Society.",
  "howToDisclaimer": "This app is a sleep aid, not a medical treatment. If insomnia persists, talk to a clinician."
